Description: Leave the scope of an application to GLib when it created one.
 gnome-session asks systemd to move each application it launches with GDesktopAppInfo into a scope of its own once
 GLib has started it. The GLib of patches/glib2.0 puts the application into that scope itself, before the application
 starts any process, and names the scope in the platform data of the "launched" signal. A request of gnome-session
 for the scope is then redundant: systemd creates the scope for whichever of the two requests it receives first, with
 the same process, and refuses the other, about which gnome-desktop's gnome_start_systemd_scope() logs a warning when
 it is gnome-session's.
Author: Mitar
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-09

diff --git a/gnome-session/gsm-util.c b/gnome-session/gsm-util.c
index f2cb616..1741bee 100644
--- a/gnome-session/gsm-util.c
+++ b/gnome-session/gsm-util.c
@@ -501,6 +501,11 @@ app_launched (GAppLaunchContext *ctx,
         if (pid == 0)
                 return;
 
+        /* GLib put the application into its own scope already, before it
+         * started */
+        if (g_variant_lookup (platform_data, "systemd-unit", "&s", NULL))
+                return;
+
         /* We are not interested in the result. */
         gnome_start_systemd_scope (g_app_info_get_id (appinfo),
                                    pid, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
